@ 17.5.1 - Proto-Indo-European to Old Irish
# citation: dhokarena56 “Laryngeal rules (the ones common to all branches except Anatolian)” Obstruent clusters assimilate in voicing to that of the final obstruent Stress change: — Pronouns, articles, and conjunctions become unstressed. — First syllables stress in all verbal imperatives. — First syllables stress in all other parts of speech except preverbs and the exceptions noted above. — Second syllables receive stress otherwise. — “This, unlike the preceding rules, remained a morphologically conditioned rule in Old Irish.” “The following three rules only apply if the vowel is unstressed”: “The following two rules apply if the vowel in question is stressed or follows the stressed syllable”; consonant clusters cannot be /nt nd/: For the following: “The book says nothing about length in the input vowels, but I think they could be either short or long from the examples given.” “The following changes. . .are, quoth the book, ‘somewhat approximative’”: — “[A] sequence of two plosives becomes a geminate of the second one” The second and third rule below “may well have been for the most part optional”; every one of the three “only applies to unstressed vowels” and “remained as a phonologically conditioned rule”:
	[+ cons, - fr, + bk, + hi, - lo]ʷ > [+ cons, - fr, + bk, + hi, - lo]
	“P:[-voice]he PI:[+front] rules for the voicing of s > z, as in:[nizdos] for *nisdos, are assumed to apply”
